WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.783
People who write many related programs

2
00:00:02.848 --> 00:00:04.220
factor out functions.

3
00:00:04.570 --> 00:00:07.082
Library learning does this automatically

4
00:00:07.082 --> 00:00:08.797
given a corpus of programs

5
00:00:08.797 --> 00:00:11.370
find the functions that make it smallest.

6
00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:13.674
BABBLE, from POPL 2023

7
00:00:13.674 --> 00:00:16.902
finds candidates by anti-unification

8
00:00:16.902 --> 00:00:19.960
and keeps the corpus in an e-graph.

9
00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:22.192
Our corpus is four turtle

10
00:00:22.258 --> 00:00:23.580
drawings of our own.

11
00:00:23.930 --> 00:00:27.370
Two are polygons, a square and a triangle.

12
00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:29.396
The other two each draw

13
00:00:29.469 --> 00:00:31.000
a polygon and a line.

14
00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:33.270
They have thirty-four nodes.

15
00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:34.656
In the e-graph

16
00:00:34.725 --> 00:00:36.729
shared fragments such as move

17
00:00:36.798 --> 00:00:39.700
ten are stored once: twenty-eight classes.

18
00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:42.204
To anti-unify two fragments

19
00:00:42.273 --> 00:00:44.128
line them up and put a hole

20
00:00:44.197 --> 00:00:45.640
wherever they differ.

21
00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:47.914
The result is the most specific

22
00:00:47.976 --> 00:00:49.590
pattern that matches both.

23
00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:53.686
The two polygons give: repeat x times

24
00:00:53.686 --> 00:00:55.460
move ten, turn y.

25
00:00:55.810 --> 00:00:58.690
That is a polygon function with two holes.

26
00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:00.697
The other two shapes differ

27
00:01:00.759 --> 00:01:03.459
right at the top, so their pattern is just a

28
00:01:03.521 --> 00:01:06.160
sequence of two holes: too general to help.

29
00:01:06.740 --> 00:01:09.220
Every pair of classes gives a candidate.

30
00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:11.884
Keeping those with at most two holes

31
00:01:11.884 --> 00:01:13.009
the task's limit

32
00:01:13.009 --> 00:01:15.010
and some structure leaves nine.

33
00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:17.710
A candidate's cost is the corpus size

34
00:01:17.773 --> 00:01:19.869
after every match becomes a call

35
00:01:19.869 --> 00:01:21.520
plus the function itself.

36
00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:24.261
The polygon pattern costs thirty-three

37
00:01:24.261 --> 00:01:26.590
because it matches only two programs.

38
00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:28.770
Pause and predict.

39
00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:30.560
Which function wins?

40
00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:37.950
The smaller one: move x, then turn y.

41
00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:40.363
It is one side of any polygon

42
00:01:40.363 --> 00:01:42.700
and it matches all four programs.

43
00:01:43.050 --> 00:01:45.023
The cost falls from thirty-four

44
00:01:45.086 --> 00:01:45.977
to thirty-one

45
00:01:45.977 --> 00:01:48.650
a reduction of eight point eight percent.

46
00:01:49.730 --> 00:01:50.829
For some domains

47
00:01:50.893 --> 00:01:52.768
the real BABBLE runs equality

48
00:01:52.833 --> 00:01:55.547
saturation first, and it finds patterns by

49
00:01:55.612 --> 00:01:56.969
crossing the e-graph

50
00:01:56.969 --> 00:01:59.490
seen as a tree automaton, with itself.

51
00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:02.320
The task removes that candidate step.

52
00:02:02.670 --> 00:02:04.647
There are ten graded corpora

53
00:02:04.647 --> 00:02:06.829
from lists and physics to text

54
00:02:06.829 --> 00:02:08.670
drawings and block towers.

55
00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:11.557
To pass, a run must compress at least

56
00:02:11.626 --> 00:02:13.340
as well as the reference

57
00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:16.220
and run strictly faster, on every corpus.

58
00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:18.922
One model passed: Claude Opus 5.5

59
00:02:18.984 --> 00:02:21.668
with a ratio of one point two eight against

60
00:02:21.730 --> 00:02:23.977
the reference's one point two seven

61
00:02:24.039 --> 00:02:26.473
in two hundred fifty-seven milliseconds

62
00:02:26.536 --> 00:02:28.720
against nine hundred seventy-three.

63
00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:32.172
The best score, nineteen point four

64
00:02:32.172 --> 00:02:34.670
went to DeepSeek V4.1 Flash.

65
00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:36.887
But its compression fell short

66
00:02:36.949 --> 00:02:38.691
on three of the ten corpora

67
00:02:38.691 --> 00:02:41.180
and it was slower on six, so it failed.

68
00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:41.984
So

69
00:02:41.984 --> 00:02:45.120
put a hole wherever two fragments differ.

70
00:02:45.470 --> 00:02:47.574
A function's value is the corpus

71
00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:50.270
size after rewriting, plus the function.

72
00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:52.538
And a run must compress as

73
00:02:52.611 --> 00:02:55.340
well and run faster, on every corpus.

74
00:02:55.690 --> 00:02:58.108
As an exercise, allow three holes

75
00:02:58.108 --> 00:03:00.170
and see which function wins.

76
00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:02.200
Next, Polygon.
